      <h1>Product-Level Verification</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Manufacturer Directory · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> A product-level entry verifies a <em>specific battery model's</em> claimed
        specification — its group size, capacity (Ah) and cold cranking amps (CCA) <em>with its test standard</em> —
        against named sources. It goes one level deeper than a company listing: it checks that the numbers on the
        label are substantiated, not just that the company exists.
      </div>

      <h2>What a product entry must state</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Group / model</strong> — the battery group or model code (e.g. H7, 55D23, N200).</li>
        <li><strong>Capacity and CCA</strong> — the claimed Ah and CCA.</li>
        <li><strong>CCA basis</strong> — which standard the CCA was measured under (JIS −15°C, EN/SAE −18°C, DIN). A CCA value without its standard is not recorded.</li>
        <li><strong>Verification source</strong> — the datasheet(s) the claim was checked against.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Example verified entries</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Alias</th><th>Standard</th><th>Voltage</th><th>Capacity</th><th>CCA</th><th>CCA basis</th><th>Verified against</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>55D23</td><td>—</td><td>JIS</td><td>12V</td><td>60 Ah</td><td>540 A</td><td>JIS (−15°C)</td><td>双帆 / 松下 / Century / GS datasheets</td></tr>
          <tr><td>65D23</td><td>—</td><td>JIS</td><td>12V</td><td>60 Ah</td><td>500 A</td><td>JIS (−15°C)</td><td>Chinese manufacturer standards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>H7</td><td>LN4 / DIN80</td><td>DIN/EN</td><td>12V</td><td>80 Ah</td><td>760 A</td><td>EN (−18°C)</td><td>Varta / Bosch / Exide datasheets</td></tr>
          <tr><td>H8</td><td>LN5 / DIN92</td><td>DIN/EN</td><td>12V</td><td>92 Ah</td><td>880 A</td><td>EN (−18°C)</td><td>Varta / Bosch / Exide datasheets</td></tr>
          <tr><td>N200</td><td>190H52</td><td>JIS</td><td>12V</td><td>200 Ah</td><td>1100 A</td><td>JIS (−15°C)</td><td>Truck battery reference datasheets</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Verified vs unverified</h2>
      <p>
        A product claim that cannot be matched to a named source is recorded as <strong>unverified</strong>, not
        silently credited. The most common place this matters is CCA — a value that omits its standard, or that reads
        higher because it was tested under the warmer JIS method, is flagged rather than passed through. See the
        <a href="/verification-criteria/">verification criteria</a> and the
        <a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">main-site CCA article</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Our Read</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My take:</strong> Product-level verification is where a directory earns its keep — a company listing
        proves the maker exists; a product entry proves the <em>spec</em> is real.</p>
        <p><strong>Supporting logic:</strong> The label number is the thing buyers actually act on, and it is exactly where
        exaggeration lives. Checking the Ah and the CCA <em>and its standard</em> against a named datasheet is the
        difference between a directory and a list.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My view as an editor, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
